# herdr-client — an agent-aware terminal multiplexer installed on THIS
# machine. Client-side: no container, nothing deployed to a remote host.
#
# Everything here is optional; the defaults work.

# --- Version pinning ---

# Which Herdr release to install. "latest" tracks upstream on every CLEAN.
#
# PIN THIS IF YOU RUN HERDR ON MORE THAN ONE MACHINE. Herdr's Machines
# feature (`herdr machine add <host>`) tolerates mismatched client/server
# versions, but an INCOMPATIBLE pair triggers an approval-based setup that
# "asks before stopping it and its pane processes, then starts the
# compatible server" — i.e. a version drift between your Mac and your Pi
# can offer to kill the remote's panes. Same version everywhere avoids it.
#
# Format: a release version like 0.9.0, or "latest".
#HERDR_VERSION=latest

# --- Session generator ---
# (scripts/generate-session.sh — also on the deploy.sh action menu)

# Where the generated Herdr session file is written. The generator reads
# each deployed agent environment's own .env for its SSH port and emits one
# pane per environment, labelled with the repo its workspace points at.
# Format: an absolute path.
#HERDR_SESSION_OUTPUT=$HOME/.config/herdr/session.generated.json

# Host the generated panes should SSH to. Leave unset for localhost, which
# is correct when Herdr runs on the same machine that hosts the containers.
#
# Note the generator emits SSH panes rather than `docker exec` panes even
# for local containers. That is deliberate: one pane shape then covers both
# local and remote, and it keeps the account running Herdr — and therefore
# Collie, and therefore your phone — pointed at an unprivileged container
# account rather than at the container runtime. See README's "Why SSH panes".
# Format: a hostname or IP.
#HERDR_TARGET_HOST=localhost
